      },
      "rev_n_2_89dc7d_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_89dc7d_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_89dc7d",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1 (revised): Instead of brute-forcing through all possible combinations of a_i and b_j, which leads to a time complexity of O(101^6) and results in a timeout, we should look for patterns or constraints that can help us determine if a solution exists more efficiently. Specifically, we can check if the differences between corresponding elements in the same row and column are constant, as this would imply that the values of a_i and b_j can be derived consistently. If these differences vary, then it's impossible to find a consistent set of a_i and b_j values that satisfy the condition for all squares in the grid.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_89dc7d_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_c8d537a872",
        "created_at": 1794501037.2681231
      },
      "rev_n_3_cd4a7c_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_cd4a7c_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_cd4a7c",
        "thought_text": "The issue lies in the logic used to determine if there exist integers \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_j\\) such that \\(c_{ij} = a_i + b_j\\). Specifically, the nested loops iterating through all possible values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are computationally expensive and may not efficiently identify a valid solution when one exists. Instead, we should leverage the properties of the grid to simplify the problem.\n\nA more efficient approach involves checking if the differences between corresponding elements in the same row and column are consistent across the entire grid. If they are, then Takahashi's statement is correct. This reduces the complexity significantly compared to brute-forcing all combinations of \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_j\\).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_cd4a7c_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_1abf970687",
        "created_at": 1794501057.6637325
      },
      "rev_n_4_83a001_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_83a001_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_83a001",
        "thought_text": "Check if the first element of each row and column is zero.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8728499487077674451",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501070.2025423
      },
      "rev_n_5_0e4936_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_0e4936_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_0e4936",
        "thought_text": "Verify if the differences between consecutive elements in each row and column are consistent.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8728499487077674451",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794501070.2025597
      },
      "rev_n_6_30b4b9_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_30b4b9_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_30b4b9",
        "thought_text": "Ensure that the minimum value in the grid is zero, as it represents the smallest possible value for both a_i and b_j.",
